== Generalization to Manifolds

On a general Riemannian manifold $(M, g)$ there is no group structure
and hence no translations.
The entire character-based construction breaks down.

However, the Laplace--Beltrami operator $Delta_g$
is intrinsically defined on any $(M, g)$.
On $RR^n$, diagonalizing $Delta$ and diagonalizing translations
yield the same eigenbasis,
since $Delta = sum_j partial_(x_j)^2$
is built from the generators of translation.

On a general manifold, we take the spectral decomposition of $Delta_g$
as the _definition_ of a generalized Fourier transform.
For compact $M$, the spectrum is discrete:
$
  Delta_g phi_k = -lambda_k phi_k, quad 0 = lambda_0 <= lambda_1 <= lambda_2 <= dots
$
and ${phi_k}$ forms an orthonormal eigenbasis for $L^2(M)$.
The eigenvalue $lambda_k$ plays the role of $abs(xi)^2$,
but the directional information encoded in $xi in RR^n$ is lost.
